      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "INTRODUCTION",
          "content": "Excessive inflammatory response is one of the major causes of early mortality in acute pancreatitis (AP)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "AIM",
          "content": "To evaluate the serum profiles of E-selectin, interleukin (IL)-6, and IL-10 along with their correlation to the markers of oxidative stress and neutrophil activation in patients with AP and patients with nonpancreatic acute abdominal pain (NPAAP)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODOLOGY",
          "content": "This prospective clinical study included 56 patients with AP (28 with mild AP and 28 with severe AP) as well as 15 patients with NPAAP."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Serum concentrations of E-selectin, IL-10, and IL-6 and plasma concentrations of polymorphonuclear leukocyte elastase (determined on days 1-3, 5, and 10 after admission) were the highest in severe AP during the first 3 days and then declined. At day 10, the E-selectin level in severe AP was still higher than that in mild AP, and the IL-10 concentration increased again. There was no elevation in the E-selectin concentration in NPAAP patients, and IL-10 levels remained unchanged in mild AP. Oxidative stress, measured by serum malondialdehyde and 4-hydroxyalkenals levels, was the most pronounced in severe AP."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "The serum E-selectin concentration is markedly elevated in severe AP and is less in mild AP but not in NPAAP. It may result from stimulation with different inflammatory mediators or indicate vascular endothelium injury mediated by oxidative stress, especially in the severe form of AP."
        }
      ]},
